 Contextualized embeddings are rate limited by total chunks, not by request count. The 16,000-chunk request limit is a structural ceiling. With an otherwise empty rate-limit window, the effective maximum is the lower of 16,000 chunks and your organization's one-second chunk allowance. Earlier requests in the window reduce the number of chunks currently available.
 
+## Context API Rate Limits
+
+Your organization's usage tier sets two Context API limits: requests per second and URLs per request. Each request counts as one unit against the requests-per-second limit, whatever its URL count, and all API keys in the organization share that limit. The URL limit applies to each request.
+
+| Tier | Requests per second | URLs per request |
+|---|---|---|
+| Tier 0 | 1 | 10 |
+| Tiers 1–3 | 3 | 15 |
+| Tiers 4–5 | 5 | 25 |
+
+Over the requests-per-second limit, the request fails with `429` and error type `request_rate_limit_exceeded`, and no URLs are fetched. Wait for the number of seconds in the `Retry-After` header, then retry. A request with more URLs than your tier allows fails with `400`, error type `invalid_request`, and `param` `urls`, without counting against your rate limit. Split it into smaller requests. No request can carry more than 50 URLs. Successful responses that went through rate limiting include `x-ratelimit-limit`, `x-ratelimit-remaining`, `x-ratelimit-used`, and `x-ratelimit-reset` (Unix seconds) headers, counted in requests. The Context API quickstart covers how requests count. If you need a higher limit, fill out our rate limit increase request form.
